research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:78:| **thm-qftx-perturbative-yang-mills-st-identity** | theorem / mathematics, or conditional physics-theorem | A separate formal order-by-order result must state the regulator/renormalization prescription, local counterterm space, field/matter content, gauge group, and anomaly hypothesis. Prove the Slavnov–Taylor identity and classify local counterterm freedom. It must not claim convergence or nonperturbative existence. |
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:84:| **post-qftx-nonabelian-lattice-regulator** | postulate / physics | Adopt one exact compact non-Abelian finite-lattice regulator with a declared action/transfer matrix and physical Gauss sector. Carry its finite-volume, lattice-spacing, matter-screening, and unit conventions. Do not assert it has a continuum limit. |
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:88:Candidate page/item assignments should also be checked against the two adjacent agents’ inventories before any canonical plan integration. The SUSY track may consume the geometric connection, non-Abelian BRST/BV, and instanton definitions where its stated action/representation hypotheses match. The anomalies/defects track may consume the local BRST cohomology definition and BV master-equation interface, but owns its anomaly polynomial/descent, global/higher anomaly, inflow, and matching results. EFT consumers may use gauge-covariant operators and the anomaly interface; they do not acquire a proof of gauge-theory renormalization or confinement.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:104:“Gauge fixing” is a choice of a slice for an action on a specified configuration space. Define the configuration-space topology/regularity (for example, Sobolev \(H^s\) connections and a Sobolev gauge group), base/boundary conditions, the stabilizer subgroup, and the subset on which the quotient action is free. A Coulomb or Lorenz condition, its differential along a gauge orbit, and a local slice require separate definitions and well-posedness hypotheses. The Faddeev–Popov operator is that derivative; it is invertible only on a local transverse slice after zero modes are removed. A determinant is ordinary finite-dimensional algebra only in a finite regulator. In a field theory functional integral it remains a formal determinant unless a regularized measure and limiting operation have been constructed.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:118:### Perturbative renormalization and anomaly control
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:120:Keep three mathematical objects separate: formal Feynman coefficients after gauge fixing; locally renormalized time-ordered products/counterterms; and a nonperturbatively defined quantum theory. A perturbative gauge-renormalization item must specify the regularization scheme, gauge parameter, ghosts, matter representation, local counterterm space, ghost number, power counting, and whether the regulator preserves BRST. Prove the Ward/Slavnov–Taylor identity order by order, not by appealing to the scalar causal-extension theorem.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:122:Local anomalies are classes in the relative local complex \(H^{1,4}(s\mid d_H)\), under a declared finite-jet algebra and locality hypothesis. The Wess–Zumino consistency equation \(s a_4+d_Ha_3=0\) is necessary; exactness means a local counterterm removes the breaking. Show that the relevant obstruction class vanishes for the specified matter representation and regulator before claiming a consistent gauge theory. A semisimple non-Abelian local anomaly, mixed \(U(1)\) anomalies, and global anomalies are distinct. The global \(SU(2)\) anomaly is not detected by a local polynomial in \(H^{1,4}(s\mid d_H)\). The anomaly-matching agent owns the physical representation/defect interface; this gauge scaffold should provide the local complex, not duplicate that output.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:124:The imported root/physics scalar results can supply only the scalar/formal algebraic ingredients actually shared by the gauge calculation. **qftx-causal-renormalization** proves scalar Epstein–Glaser induction and finite renormalization composition; **qftx-renormalization-hopf** proves a combinatorial rooted-forest subtraction factorization. Neither proves non-Abelian gauge invariance of counterterms or absence of anomalies. The 1973 asymptotic-freedom papers establish a perturbative ultraviolet result under their model assumptions; they do not prove the infrared theory, confinement, a mass gap, or convergence of the perturbation series.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:132:Any semiclassical instanton contribution to a path integral additionally needs gauge zero-mode handling, the fluctuation determinant and its regulator/renormalization, collective-coordinate measure, ghost determinant, and any fermion zero modes and selection rules. If these are only a saddle expansion, label it formal/asymptotic and state the expansion parameter and domain. An instanton is a classical Euclidean solution; it is not a proof of the mass gap or confinement.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:134:### Lattice regulators, Wilson lines, and confinement
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:136:For a new non-Abelian lattice model, define a finite cell complex or graph, \(G\)-valued edge variables, vertex gauge action, ordered plaquette holonomy, Haar integration or the Hamiltonian link Hilbert space \(L^2(G)\), the exact gauge-invariant action/transfer matrix, and Gauss operators. Prove the finite-regulator gauge invariance and physical subspace separately. The imported compact \(U(1)\) rotor/CAR system is a useful analogy but is not a non-Abelian link model.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:144:The one proposed open item is **op-qftx-clay-4d-yang-mills-existence-mass-gap**, classified as mathematics and nonlogical. The official Jaffe–Witten problem states: for every compact simple gauge group \(G\), prove existence of a nontrivial quantum Yang–Mills theory on \(\mathbb R^4\) with a positive mass gap \(\Delta>0\), with existence axioms at least as strong as Wightman or Osterwalder–Schrader. In that source, a mass gap means the Hamiltonian has no spectrum in \((0,\Delta)\) above the vacuum. The current Clay “Unsolved” archive still lists “Yang-Mills & the Mass Gap,” checked 2026-10-23. The item’s Status should preserve that date/source, cite partial finite-lattice/other-dimensional/regulator results without overgeneralizing, and state what remains required. It is not a dependency or a premise.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:146:The official Clay formulation does not ask for a proof of the Wilson-loop area law or color confinement. Jaffe–Witten discuss mass gap, confinement, and chiral symmetry breaking as separate desired quantum properties, then call confinement a natural extension of the prize problem. Keep these formulations separate. This scaffold does not create an op-item for “confinement” until the exact observable, group, matter content, limit, and current status have their own source review. A theorem awaiting local proof (classical Yang–Mills variation, BPST verification, local slice theorem, finite-lattice strong-coupling expansion) remains an authoring obligation and must not be mislabeled open.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:160:2. **Arthur Jaffe and Edward Witten, “Quantum Yang–Mills Theory,” official Clay problem description (2000).** Read the Clay-hosted 14-page PDF, especially §§1–2, §4 “The Problem,” and §5–6. It gives the compact-group classical curvature/action conventions, distinguishes the three quantum properties mass gap, confinement and chiral symmetry breaking, defines the mass-gap spectral condition, and states the exact compact-simple-\(G\), \(\mathbb R^4\) existence-and-gap problem. Its problem text describes mathematical construction as incomplete; the separate current Clay “Unsolved Archives” page was checked 2026-10-23. This is the authoritative problem formulation/status source, not a proof that any physical model has the stated properties.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:162:3. **Glenn Barnich, Friedemann Brandt, Marc Henneaux, “Local BRST cohomology in gauge theories,” Physics Reports 338 (2000), arXiv:hep-th/0002245.** Targeted reading of the abstract, §2.2 (Yang–Mills-type field content and BRST transformations), §2.3 (nilpotence, \(H(s)\), \(H(s\mid d)\)), §2.7 (contractible nonminimal pairs), §11 (the stated cohomology theorem for Yang–Mills-type theories without free Abelian gauge symmetries, in \(n>2\)), and §12.3 (ghost-number-one anomaly classes). The text defines a specific class of local theories: matter transforms linearly in a completely reducible representation; the Lagrangian is local and gauge invariant up to a divergence; additional matter gauge symmetries are excluded for that analysis. Its local anomaly equation is \(sa+dm=0\), with counterterms removing trivial classes. The review’s theorems have technical hypotheses and selected cases; this targeted reading is not a proof reconstruction or a blanket anomaly-cancellation theorem for any gauge/matter model. The new items must reproduce the exact chosen subcase and proof locally.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:170:7. **David J. Gross and Frank Wilczek, “Ultraviolet Behavior of Non-Abelian Gauge Theories,” Physical Review Letters 30 (1973), 1343–1346; H. David Politzer, “Reliable Perturbative Results for Strong Interactions?,” Physical Review Letters 30 (1973), 1346–1349.** Read the APS abstracts only. Gross–Wilczek state that a wide class of non-Abelian gauge theories have free-field ultraviolet asymptotic behavior up to calculable logarithmic corrections. Politzer states a perturbative result for deep-Euclidean Green functions of Yang–Mills theories and specified fermionic extensions. The exact one-/higher-loop coefficients, scheme assumptions, and counterterms are not imported from the abstracts; derive or source them in the perturbative renormalization branch. These statements say nothing by themselves about the infrared or a convergent perturbation series.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-gauge.md:172:8. **G. ’t Hooft and M. Veltman, “Regularization and Renormalization of Gauge Fields,” Nuclear Physics B 44 (1972), 189–213.** Bibliographic record and abstract inspected; the full paper was not read for this scaffold. It presents dimensional regularization/renormalization methods for gauge fields and Yang–Mills-type theories. Cite it as a source to study, not as the proof of the new Slavnov–Taylor or anomaly statements.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-symmetries-defects-anomalies.md:1:# Generalized symmetries, defects, gauging, and anomalies
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-symmetries-defects-anomalies.md:3:Research note dated 2026-10-23 for the new physics category **advanced-qft-and-supersymmetry**. This is a source-backed prose design for the generalized-symmetry, defect, and anomaly strand. It records current local suppliers, candidate interfaces, proof obligations, and a bounded research frontier. It does not add production items, establish a physical model, certify a proof, or claim an exhaustive October 2026 literature review. The engine and publication steps remain separate.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-symmetries-defects-anomalies.md:5:The companion subject notes own the full non-Abelian gauge-theory and BRST/BV constructions, general EFT matching and experiment analyses, and the supersymmetry and localization/BPS models. This note gives those strands precise symmetry, defect, and anomaly interfaces. Reuse the EFT proposal **def-aqft-anomaly-matching-data** as the shared anomaly-matching interface if the builder keeps it; do not author a competing definition with a different scope. The gauge researcher proposed **def-qftx-nonabelian-brst-complex**, **lem-qftx-nonabelian-brst-nilpotence**, **def-qftx-bv-antibracket-master-action**, and **lem-qftx-bv-master-equation-brst-nilpotence**. These are planned suppliers, not current results, and a consumer may use them only after their exact contracts and proof status are checked.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-symmetries-defects-anomalies.md:15:A 2-group with 0-form group \(G\), Abelian 1-form group \(A\), action \(\rho:G\to\mathrm{Aut}(A)\), and Postnikov class \([\beta]\in H^3_\rho(BG;A)\) has a coupled background. For a discrete model, a flat 1-cochain \(a\) and a 2-cochain \(B\) obey a twisted equation of the form \(\delta_a B=a^*\beta\). Its gauge transformations, cup-product convention, local coefficients, and cochain representatives must be supplied. The Postnikov class says that the two background gauge fields are not independent. It is not, by itself, a ’t Hooft anomaly. General \(n\)-groups and continuous backgrounds need their own definitions and should not be inferred from this 2-group contract.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-symmetries-defects-anomalies.md:23:Coupling a symmetry to a **background** means evaluating the theory with a nondynamical gauge field or defect network and keeping its gauge transformations visible. **Gauging** makes this background dynamical and sums/integrates over equivalence classes with the correct gauge-volume or automorphism weights and any allowed topological action. Gauging requires that the full background variation be removable by allowed local counterterms, or that the theory be specified relative to an inflow bulk. For finite group models, the cocycle action and the precise groupoid of fields/gauge transformations are part of the input. A finite-dimensional state sum does not prove existence of a continuum gauge theory.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-symmetries-defects-anomalies.md:25:A **’t Hooft anomaly** is the residual transformation of the partition function under background gauge transformations after all allowed \(d\)-dimensional local counterterms have been considered. This makes the partition function a section of an anomaly line, or equivalently a boundary object relative to an invertible \((d+1)\)-dimensional anomaly theory when such a description is available. It obstructs gauging the global symmetry and is preserved along a symmetry-preserving renormalization-group flow under the stated locality and background-coupling assumptions. It is different from a gauge anomaly of a dynamical gauge redundancy, which obstructs defining the proposed gauge theory unless a specified cancellation mechanism is supplied. It is also different from explicit symmetry breaking, a higher-group background constraint, and a nonconservation equation that is an operator-valued Ward identity rather than a c-number anomaly.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-symmetries-defects-anomalies.md:27:Separate at least four anomaly contracts:
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-symmetries-defects-anomalies.md:29:1. **Perturbative/local anomalies** are infinitesimal background-gauge variations represented by local descent data. A selected \(d\)-dimensional chiral theory may have an invariant polynomial \(I_{d+2}\), a descent \(I_{d+2}=d I_{d+1}^{(0)}\), \(\delta I_{d+1}^{(0)}=d I_d^{(1)}\), and a variation proportional to \(\int_M I_d^{(1)}\). Fix anti-Hermitian versus Hermitian connections, trace normalization, chirality, \(2\pi\) and \(i\) conventions, the regulator, and admissible counterterms. In four dimensions the gauge and mixed gauge-gravitational terms live in degree six, but no coefficient follows from a name such as “chiral fermion” without the representations and the chosen normalization.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-symmetries-defects-anomalies.md:30:2. **Global anomalies** concern large or disconnected gauge transformations and can remain when the local anomaly polynomial vanishes. State the gauge-transformation group, spin/orientation data, determinant-line or eta-invariant convention, and exact background family. A perturbative descent calculation does not dispose of torsion or global phases.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-symmetries-defects-anomalies.md:31:3. **Mixed/higher-group anomalies** involve several backgrounds whose gauge transformations are coupled. Write the full background data and transformations before computing the variation. A characteristic differential form alone may miss torsion; ordinary \(H^{d+1}(BG;U(1))\) is a useful finite bosonic group-like model, not a universal classification of fermionic, higher-form, or categorical anomalies.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-symmetries-defects-anomalies.md:32:4. **Categorical/noninvertible anomalies** depend on defect fusion, associators, junctions, and background or gauging data for that exact higher category. Do not label every obstruction to a chosen gauging as an anomaly field theory until the local and global obstruction, counterterm equivalence, and possible inflow have all been specified.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-symmetries-defects-anomalies.md:34:Anomaly inflow is a conditional cancellation theorem: the boundary variation and the bulk variation must be evaluated in the same convention on manifolds with boundary, including the same tangential structure and background fields, and their product must be gauge invariant under all admitted transformations. Anomaly matching is likewise conditional: if the exact global symmetry is preserved along the RG flow, its anomaly class is unchanged modulo the declared counterterms. A unique trivially gapped symmetric IR phase is excluded only after the symmetry, anomaly, and allowed topological sectors have been fixed; gapless modes, symmetry breaking, topological order, or a relative boundary theory can match the anomaly.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-symmetries-defects-anomalies.md:36:For supersymmetry, distinguish fermionic super-Poincaré generators from internal 0-form symmetries and from higher-form charges. A physical supercharge requires a real Lorentzian form, spinor representation, adjoint operation, common operator domain, Hamiltonian, and a positive-energy Hilbert-space representation. Central charges and brane charges may yield BPS bounds but do not automatically define a generalized global symmetry. An \(R\)-symmetry is an internal global symmetry with its own background/anomaly data; the supercharges themselves are not gaugeable as an ordinary internal group. A supersymmetric anomaly multiplet, supersymmetric inflow action, defect-preserving superalgebra, Witten index, or localization formula needs its own exact model and hypotheses. In particular, a trace index requires trace-class or other controlled spectral hypotheses and a proof that continuum/boundary terms do not invalidate the pairing argument.
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-symmetries-defects-anomalies.md:44:| **physics/library/quantum-field-theory/qft-c01.md**: **def-qft-framework-units**, **post-qft-constructed-model-and-measurement**; **qft-g01.md**: **post-qft-free-photon-model**, **post-qft-qed-local-coupling**; **qft-g02.md**: **post-qft-formal-qed-coefficients**, **post-qft-compact-lattice-gauge-model**, related finite/formal theorems | The selected Minkowski length-coordinate and SI/natural-unit dictionary; a positive transverse free photon model; a smooth classical charged Dirac/Maxwell coupling; and a separate finite graph/formal QED branch. | **post-qft-qed-local-coupling** explicitly uses compactly supported local gauge variations and does not cover large gauge transformations, nontrivial bundles, boundary charges, asymptotic sectors, or an interacting continuum Hilbert space. **qft-g02** does not assert formal-series convergence or a continuum limit. These pages are not suppliers for global anomalies. |
