research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-12.pages.json:86:        "statement": "Fix integers $N\\geq1$ and $k\\geq0$ and a positive real stability parameter $\\zeta$. Let $V=\\mathbb C^k$ and $W=\\mathbb C^N$ have their standard Hermitian structures. Define $\\mathcal M^\\zeta_{k,N}$ to be the geometric quotient by $GL(V)$ of data $(B_1,B_2,I,J)$ with $B_i\\in\\operatorname{End}(V)$, $I\\in\\operatorname{Hom}(W,V)$, $J\\in\\operatorname{Hom}(V,W)$, $[B_1,B_2]+IJ=0$, and $\\operatorname{span}\\{B_{i_1}\\cdots B_{i_m}I(w):m\\geq0,\\ w\\in W\\}=V$. The positive parameter $\\zeta$ specifies the positive-FI stability chamber. Let $T=(S^1)^2\\times(S^1)^N$, with rotation characters $t_1,t_2$ and dual framing characters $e_1,\\ldots,e_N$. Thus $W$ has character $\\mathbf W=\\sum_\\alpha e_\\alpha^{-1}$ and $W^\\vee$ has character $\\mathbf W^\\vee=\\sum_\\alpha e_\\alpha$. Its action is $(t_1,t_2,h)\\cdot(B_1,B_2,I,J)=(t_1B_1,t_2B_2,Ih^{-1},t_1t_2hJ)$, where $h$ acts on $W$ with weights $e_\\alpha^{-1}$. Complexify the characters and work over the rational function field $K=\\mathbb Q(\\epsilon_1,\\epsilon_2,a_1,\\ldots,a_N)$, where $\\epsilon_i=c_1^T(t_i)$ and $a_\\alpha=c_1^T(e_\\alpha)$.\n\nFor a partition $Y$, a Young diagram means a finite downward-closed subset of $\\mathbb Z_{\\geq0}^2$; write $|Y|$ for its number of boxes. For an $N$-tuple $\\vec Y=(Y_1,\\ldots,Y_N)$ set $|\\vec Y|=\\sum_\\alpha|Y_\\alpha|$, $\\mathbf W=\\sum_\\alpha e_\\alpha^{-1}$, $\\mathbf W^\\vee=\\sum_\\alpha e_\\alpha$, and\n$$\\mathbf V_{\\vec Y}=\\sum_{\\alpha=1}^N e_\\alpha\\sum_{(i,j)\\in Y_\\alpha}t_1^it_2^j.$$\nThen $\\mathcal M^\\zeta_{k,N}$ is smooth of complex dimension $2kN$, and its $T$-fixed points are isolated and in bijection with the $N$-tuples of partitions of total size $k$. At the fixed point $\\vec Y$, the tangent character is\n$$\\operatorname{ch}_T(T_{\\vec Y}\\mathcal M^\\zeta_{k,N})=\\mathbf V_{\\vec Y}^{\\vee}\\mathbf V_{\\vec Y}(t_1+t_2-1-t_1t_2)+\\mathbf V_{\\vec Y}^{\\vee}\\mathbf W^\\vee+t_1t_2\\mathbf W\\mathbf V_{\\vec Y}.$$\nFor each character $\\chi$, let $u_\\chi=c_1^T(\\chi)$. Let $E_{\\vec Y}$ be the product of $u_\\chi$ over the tangent weights counted with multiplicity; the fixed points are isolated, so none of these weights is zero. Define the fixed-point series by\n$$Z^{\\mathrm{fp}}_N(q)=\\sum_{k\\geq0}q^k\\sum_{|\\vec Y|=k}E_{\\vec Y}^{-1}\\in K\\langle\\!\\langle q\\rangle\\!\\rangle.$$\nEach coefficient is a finite sum, and the series is formal; no analytic convergence is asserted.\n\nAssume full AC as inherited from the supplied instanton-charge convention. If a $T$-equivariant isomorphism from the positive-FI quotient to the moduli of framed rank-$N$ torsion-free coherent sheaves on $\\mathbb{CP}^2$ with Hilbert polynomial $N\\binom{m+2}{2}-k$ is supplied, it transfers the fixed-point classification and tangent characters to that Gieseker space. The ordinary framed anti-self-dual moduli space is a separate zero-FI regular locus: identifying that locus with the regular ADHM quotient is another unresolved conditional input, and no fixed-point count or equality of series is asserted for it. If a separately proved noncompact equivariant pushforward for $\\mathcal M^\\zeta_{k,N}$ has a localization identity with these isolated fixed points and tangent Euler classes, then its value on the unit is the coefficient displayed above. The compact ABBV formula alone does not establish such an identity: $\\mathcal M^\\zeta_{k,N}$ is noncompact for $k>0$. This theorem makes no identification with an ordinary continuum gauge-theory path integral.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-12.pages.json:140:        "statement": "Let $\\mathfrak M^\\zeta_{1,1}$ be the quotient of $V=W=\\mathbb C$ data $(B_1,B_2,I,J)$ satisfying $[B_1,B_2]+IJ=0$ and $\\operatorname{span}\\{B_1^{m_1}B_2^{m_2}I(W):m_1,m_2\\geq0\\}=V$, modulo $GL(V)$, in the positive stability chamber. Let $t_1,t_2$ be the two rotation characters and set $\\epsilon_i=c_1^T(t_i)$. The datum $B_1=B_2=J=0$, $I=1$ is stable and satisfies the complex moment-map equation. The unique fixed point is the one-box partition $Y_1=\\{(0,0)\\}$; its tangent character is $t_1+t_2$, its equivariant Euler class is $\\epsilon_1\\epsilon_2$, and its contribution to the degree-one coefficient of the fixed-point series is $(\\epsilon_1\\epsilon_2)^{-1}$.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-12.pages.json:171:        "statement": "Let $\\mathfrak M^\\zeta_{1,2}$ be the quotient of $V=\\mathbb C$, $W=\\mathbb C^2$ data $(B_1,B_2,I,J)$ satisfying $[B_1,B_2]+IJ=0$ and $\\operatorname{span}\\{B_1^{m_1}B_2^{m_2}I(W):m_1,m_2\\geq0\\}=V$, modulo $GL(V)$, in the positive stability chamber. Let $t_1,t_2$ be the rotation characters, let $e_1,e_2$ be the dual framing characters, and put $\\epsilon_i=c_1^T(t_i)$ and $a_\\alpha=c_1^T(e_\\alpha)$. The two fixed points are $(\\{(0,0)\\},\\varnothing)$ and $(\\varnothing,\\{(0,0)\\})$. Their tangent characters are respectively\n$$t_1+t_2+\\frac{e_2}{e_1}+t_1t_2\\frac{e_1}{e_2}\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad t_1+t_2+\\frac{e_1}{e_2}+t_1t_2\\frac{e_2}{e_1}.$$\nTheir Euler classes are\n$$E_1=\\epsilon_1\\epsilon_2(a_2-a_1)(\\epsilon_1+\\epsilon_2+a_1-a_2),\\qquad E_2=\\epsilon_1\\epsilon_2(a_1-a_2)(\\epsilon_1+\\epsilon_2+a_2-a_1).$$\nThus the degree-one coefficient of the fixed-point series is $E_1^{-1}+E_2^{-1}$.\n\nThe diagram records the two partition tuples; each square is one one-dimensional weight space of $V$, and the subscript identifies the framing color.\n\n~~~tikz\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.75cm,y=0.75cm]\n  \\draw[fill=blue!15] (0,0) rectangle (1,1);\n  \\draw (0,0) grid (1,1);\n  \\node[below] at (0.5,0) {$Y_1$};\n  \\node[below] at (2,0) {$Y_2=\\varnothing$};\n  \\draw[fill=orange!20] (4,0) rectangle (5,1);\n  \\draw (4,0) grid (5,1);\n  \\node[below] at (4.5,0) {$Y_2$};\n  \\node[below] at (6,0) {$Y_1=\\varnothing$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n~~~",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-47.pages.json:139:        "statement": "Adopt the following Euclidean source model in the conventions stated here. Let $r>0$ have units of length and let\n$$S^4_r=\\{y\\in\\mathbb R^5:\\sum_{j=1}^5 y_j^2=r^2\\}$$\nhave its round metric, standard orientation, and scalar curvature $12/r^2$. In stereographic coordinates $x^\\mu$ of length, $\\mu=1,\\dots,4$, the metric is\n$$ds^2=\\frac{\\sum_{\\mu=1}^4(dx^\\mu)^2}{(1+x^2/(4r^2))^2},\\qquad x^2=\\sum_{\\mu=1}^4(x^\\mu)^2.$$\nUse the standard spin structure, a trivial $SU(2)_R$ background bundle with zero connection, and the $SU(2)_R$ generator selected below. The global gauge group is $G=SU(2)$, not $SO(3)$; its Lie algebra consists of anti-Hermitian $2\\times2$ traceless matrices and has positive invariant form $\\kappa(X,Y)=-2\\operatorname{Tr}_2(XY)$, normalized by $\\kappa(-i\\sigma_a/2,-i\\sigma_b/2)=\\delta_{ab}$. Let $\\Sigma$ be the declared set of isomorphism classes of smooth right principal $SU(2)$-bundles over $S^4_r$; each $P\\in\\Sigma$ is a separate gauge sector. The sector set and any sum over it are formal model inputs, not a classification or measure construction.\n\nThe matter content is the massless four-dimensional $\\mathcal N=4$ vector multiplet: one gauge connection $A_\\mu$, six real adjoint scalar fields $\\Phi_A$ with $A\\in\\{0,5,6,7,8,9\\}$, and four adjoint Weyl fermions. Equivalently, its flat-space component content is one $\\mathcal N=1$ vector multiplet and three adjoint $\\mathcal N=1$ chiral multiplets. Set the topological $\\theta$-term to zero in this selected model. Use the ordinary trace $\\operatorname{Tr}_2$ in the defining representation of $SU(2)$ and a positive dimensionless coupling $g_{\\mathrm{YM}}$. The current N=4 model carrier uses the same global group, $\\kappa(X,Y)=-2\\operatorname{Tr}_2(XY)$, the same dimensionless coupling, and connection-normalized adjoint chiral fields, with one vector and three adjoint chiral multiplets; this is an interface match for flat-space component content and normalization. This postulate separately adopts the source’s curved $S^4$ action, curvature coupling, selected-spinor algebra, gauge fixing, contour, and formal regulator inputs; it does not identify that curved action with the supplier’s flat Minkowski superspace action. For the dimensional-reduction notation, in a local gauge trivialization define the Lie-algebra-valued components $F_{\\mu\\nu}=\\partial_\\mu A_\\nu-\\partial_\\nu A_\\mu+[A_\\mu,A_\\nu]$, $F_{\\mu A}=D_\\mu\\Phi_A$, and $F_{AB}=[\\Phi_A,\\Phi_B]$, extended antisymmetrically to $F_{MN}$. Here $D_\\mu X=\\nabla_\\mu^{\\mathrm{LC}}X+[A_\\mu,X]$ on adjoint tensor fields, with the supplied spin connection on spinor indices, and $D_A X=[\\Phi_A,X]$; in the fermion term $D_\\mu\\Psi$ includes the spin and gauge connections. These $F_{MN}$ are Lie-algebra-valued matrices, so $\\operatorname{Tr}_2$ is taken in the defining representation. Use $\\eta_{MN}=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,\\dots,1)$ for $M,N=0,\\dots,9$. With the seven localization auxiliaries included, the adopted classical action input is\n$$S_{\\mathrm{off}}=-\\frac{1}{g_{\\mathrm{YM}}^2}\\int_{S^4_r}d^4x\\,\\sqrt g\\,\\operatorname{Tr}_2\\left(\\frac12\\sum_{M,N}F_{MN}F^{MN}-\\Psi\\Gamma^M D_M\\Psi+\\frac{2}{r^2}\\sum_A\\Phi_A\\Phi^A-\\sum_{i=1}^7K_iK_i\\right).$$\nHere $\\Psi$ is the adjoint ten-dimensional Majorana–Weyl fermion used to package the four-dimensional fields; after Euclidean continuation it is complex and has no independent conjugate field. The chiral gamma matrices obey $\\Gamma_M\\widetilde\\Gamma_N+\\Gamma_N\\widetilde\\Gamma_M=2\\eta_{MN}I_{16}$, with $\\widetilde\\Gamma_0=-\\Gamma_0$ and $\\widetilde\\Gamma_M=\\Gamma_M$ for $M=1,\\dots,9$; $\\Gamma_{MN}:=\\widetilde\\Gamma_{[M}\\Gamma_{N]}$. The four sphere directions have positive metric. The term with $2/r^2$ is the conformal curvature coupling of the six scalars. The Yang–Mills coupling is dimensionless, $[A_\\mu]=[\\Phi_A]=\\mathrm{length}^{-1}$, $[K_i]=[F_{MN}]=\\mathrm{length}^{-2}$, and $[\\Psi]=\\mathrm{length}^{-3/2}$.\n\nLet $\\widehat\\epsilon_s=(0_8,1,0_7)^{\\mathsf T}$ be the normalized sixteen-component chiral spinor in the source gamma convention, with $\\Gamma_{5678}\\widehat\\epsilon_s=\\widehat\\epsilon_s$ and $\\Gamma_{1234}\\widehat\\epsilon_s=-\\widehat\\epsilon_s$, and set $\\widehat\\epsilon_c=(2r)^{-1}\\Gamma_{012}\\widehat\\epsilon_s$. With $\\Omega(x)=1+x^2/(4r^2)$, adopt\n$$\\epsilon(x)=\\Omega(x)^{-1/2}\\bigl(\\widehat\\epsilon_s+x^i\\widetilde\\Gamma_i\\widehat\\epsilon_c\\bigr),\\qquad \\widetilde\\epsilon(x)=\\Omega(x)^{-1/2}\\left(\\widehat\\epsilon_c-\\frac{x^i}{4r^2}\\Gamma_i\\widehat\\epsilon_s\\right),\\quad i=1,\\dots,4.$$\nThey solve the conformal Killing-spinor system\n$$\\nabla_\\mu\\epsilon=\\widetilde\\Gamma_\\mu\\widetilde\\epsilon,\\qquad \\nabla_\\mu\\widetilde\\epsilon=-\\frac{1}{4r^2}\\Gamma_\\mu\\epsilon.$$\nThe selected spinors obey the source's same-chirality conditions, in particular $\\widetilde\\epsilon\\epsilon=0$, so the dilation term in the supercharge square vanishes. Their bilinear $v^\\mu=\\epsilon\\Gamma^\\mu\\epsilon$ generates the $U(1)$ isometry rotating the $(x^1,x^2)$ and $(x^3,x^4)$ planes equally. The corresponding internal R-symmetry generator is $R_{AB}=2\\widetilde\\epsilon\\Gamma_{AB}\\epsilon$ on scalar fields and its induced spin representation on fermions.\n\nFor this single supercharge, introduce seven adjoint auxiliary scalars $K_i$, $i=1,\\dots,7$, and seven spinors $\\nu_i$ satisfying\n$$\\epsilon\\Gamma_M\\nu_i=0,\\qquad \\frac12(\\epsilon\\Gamma^N\\epsilon)\\widetilde\\Gamma_{N,\\alpha\\beta}=\\sum_{i=1}^7\\nu_{i,\\alpha}\\nu_{i,\\beta}+\\epsilon_\\alpha\\epsilon_\\beta,\\qquad \\nu_i\\Gamma_M\\nu_j=\\delta_{ij}\\epsilon\\Gamma_M\\epsilon.$$\nAdopt the off-shell transformations\n$$Q_\\epsilon A_M=\\Psi\\Gamma_M\\epsilon,\\qquad Q_\\epsilon\\Psi=\\frac12\\Gamma^{MN}F_{MN}\\epsilon+\\frac12\\Gamma^\\mu\\Gamma^A\\Phi_A\\nabla_\\mu\\epsilon+\\sum_iK_i\\nu_i,\\qquad Q_\\epsilon K_i=-\\nu_i\\Gamma^M D_M\\Psi.$$\nWith $v^M=\\epsilon\\Gamma^M\\epsilon$, their square is the source's off-shell symmetry\n$$Q_\\epsilon^2=-\\mathcal L_v-\\delta_{\\mathrm{gauge}}(v^M A_M)-\\delta_R-\\delta_\\Omega,$$\nwhere $\\mathcal L_v$ is the lifted Lie derivative along the spacetime part $v^\\mu$, $\\delta_{\\mathrm{gauge}}(u)$ acts on an adjoint field $X$ by $[u,X]$ and on a connection by $-D_\\mu u$, $\\delta_R$ is the internal transformation generated by $R_{AB}$, and $\\delta_\\Omega$ is the dilation with parameter $2\\widetilde\\epsilon\\epsilon=0$. This off-shell closure is for the selected generator; it is not a claim that the full hypermultiplet algebra closes with finitely many auxiliary fields.\n\nIn a trivialization chart use the Faddeev–Popov gauge slice $d^*A=0$, where $d^*$ is the metric adjoint of the exterior derivative. In a nontrivial bundle sector $P$, choose a background connection $A_*$ and fluctuations $a=A-A_*$ in $\\Omega^1(S^4,\\operatorname{ad}P)$; the supplied local slice is $d_{A_*}^*a=0$ and its Faddeev–Popov operator is $d_{A_*}^*D_A$ with stabilizer modes omitted. Introduce odd ghost and antighost fields $c,\\widetilde c$, an even multiplier $b$, and the constant zero-mode fields $a_0,\\widetilde a_0,b_0,c_0,\\widetilde c_0$. In the trivialization chart the Faddeev–Popov operator is $d^*D_{A_{\\mathrm{gauge}}}$ with its zero modes omitted; its determinant is denoted $\\det'(d^*D_{A_{\\mathrm{gauge}}})$. Take dimensionless gauge-fixing parameters $\\xi_1,\\xi_2>0$ and the BRST gauge-fixing functional\n$$\\delta\\left((\\widetilde c,\\,i d^*A+\\tfrac{\\xi_1}{2}b+i b_0)-(c,\\,\\widetilde a_0-\\tfrac{\\xi_2}{2}a_0)\\right).$$\nThe brackets are the integrated invariant pairing on adjoint-valued fields, and the prime removes the stabilizer/constant zero modes treated by the displayed auxiliary fields. Let $S^\\delta_{\\mathrm{gf}}$ denote the BRST variation of this functional. If $\\psi$ is the fermionic one-form superpartner of $A$ and $\\rho_{\\mathrm{gh}}(\\omega)$ is the infinitesimal action of the selected circle generator on ghosts, the source's $Q$-closed completion is\n$$S^Q_{\\mathrm{gf}}=S^\\delta_{\\mathrm{gf}}-(\\widetilde c,d^*\\psi+\\tfrac{\\xi_1}{2}\\rho_{\\mathrm{gh}}(\\omega)\\widetilde c)-(\\phi,\\widetilde a_0-\\tfrac{\\xi_2}{2}a_0).$$\nThe gauge-fixed functional integral is the formal expression\n$$Z_{\\mathrm{formal}}=\\sum_{[P]\\in\\Sigma}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{vol}(\\mathcal G(P),g_{\\mathrm{YM}})} \\int_{\\mathcal C_P}[D\\Phi\\,Dc\\,D\\widetilde c\\,Db\\,D(\\text{zero modes})]\\, e^{-S_{\\mathrm{off}}[P]-S_{\\mathrm{gf}}^Q[P]}.$$\nHere $\\mathcal G(P)$ is the smooth gauge-automorphism group of $P$, $\\mathcal C_P$ is the declared contour in that sector, and $\\operatorname{vol}(\\mathcal G(P),g_{\\mathrm{YM}})$ is the source's formal gauge-group volume with its invariant measure scaled by the gauge coupling; it is not an actual infinite-dimensional Haar measure. In the Euclidean contour, gauge fields and $\\Phi_{5},\\dots,\\Phi_9$ lie in the real Lie algebra, $\\Phi_0=i\\Phi_0^E$ with $\\Phi_0^E$ real, the seven localization auxiliaries satisfy $K_i=iK_i^E$ with $K_i^E$ real, and $a_0=i a_0^E$ with $a_0^E$ real.\n\nLet $\\delta_{\\mathrm{gauge}}(u)$ denote the infinitesimal gauge action with parameter $u$, and let $\\delta_{U(1)}(\\omega)$ denote the lifted infinitesimal circle action. The supersymmetry differential acts by $sX=X'$, $sX'=\\delta_{\\mathrm{gauge}}(\\phi)X+\\delta_{U(1)}(\\omega)X$, $sc=\\phi$, $s\\phi=0$, $sa_0=0$, $s\\widetilde c=s\\widetilde a_0=sb_0=s\\widetilde c_0=sc_0=0$, and $sb=\\rho_{\\mathrm{gh}}(\\omega)\\widetilde c$. The Faddeev–Popov differential acts on an adjoint-valued tensor field $X$ by $\\delta X=-[c,X]$ and on its odd superpartner by $\\delta X'=-[c,X']$. On the gauge connection it acts by $\\delta A_\\mu=D_\\mu c=-\\delta_{\\mathrm{gauge}}(c)A_\\mu$, using the earlier convention $\\delta_{\\mathrm{gauge}}(u)A_\\mu=-D_\\mu u$; the connection's one-form superpartner $\\psi$ transforms as $\\delta\\psi=-[c,\\psi]$. The remaining rules are $\\delta c=-a_0-\\tfrac12[c,c]$, $\\delta a_0=0$, $\\delta\\phi=-[c,\\phi]-\\delta_{U(1)}(\\omega)\\phi$, $\\delta\\widetilde c=b$, $\\delta b=[a_0,\\widetilde c]$, $\\delta\\widetilde a_0=\\widetilde c_0$, $\\delta\\widetilde c_0=[a_0,\\widetilde a_0]$, $\\delta b_0=c_0$, and $\\delta c_0=[a_0,b_0]$. Here $X$ ranges over adjoint-valued physical and auxiliary tensor fields other than the connection, $X'$ is its odd superpartner, $c,\\widetilde c,c_0,\\widetilde c_0$ are odd, and $a_0,\\widetilde a_0,b,b_0,\\phi$ are even. The total localization differential is $Q=s+\\delta$, with $s$ the supersymmetry differential and $\\delta$ the Faddeev–Popov differential. These rules give, on adjoint-valued tensor fields and their superpartners,\n$$s^2X'=\\delta_{\\mathrm{gauge}}(\\phi)X'+\\delta_{U(1)}(\\omega)X',\\qquad \\delta^2X=\\delta_{\\mathrm{gauge}}(a_0)X,\\qquad \\{s,\\delta\\}X=-\\delta_{\\mathrm{gauge}}(\\phi)X,$$\nand the same graded algebra on the connection and its one-form superpartner when the gauge action on the connection is interpreted as $\\delta_{\\mathrm{gauge}}(u)A_\\mu=-D_\\mu u$. On ghosts, $\\{s,\\delta\\}=\\delta_{U(1)}(\\omega)$ and $s^2=0$; the displayed ghost transformations give $\\delta^2=\\delta_{\\mathrm{gauge}}(a_0)$. Thus $Q^2=\\delta_{\\mathrm{gauge}}(a_0)+\\delta_{U(1)}(\\omega)$ on fields and ghosts: a constant gauge transformation plus the $U(1)$ spacetime rotation. These are the finite-dimensional geometric symmetry data of the background; this assertion does not identify the infinite-dimensional field space with a smooth finite-dimensional manifold.\n\nAdopt as a formal regulator the supersymmetry-compatible noncommutative deformation used by the source for gauge-field regularization on $\\mathbb R^4$. Its $\\Omega$-background is the $U(1)^2$ action rotating the two coordinate planes in $\\mathbb R^4\\cong\\mathbb C^2$, with angular parameters $\\varepsilon_1=\\varepsilon_2=r^{-1}$, each of mass dimension one. For the four-sphere, assume an extension exists and agrees near the north and south poles with the stated $\\mathbb R^4$ regulator. This extension is not explicitly constructed in the source and is an additional unresolved model assumption. The regulated measure, contour, and gauge-fixed action are likewise formal inputs; any use of $S_t=S+tQV$ is conditional on measure and contour invariance, no anomalous Jacobian, justified differentiation under the integral, and no uncontrolled boundary contribution. This postulate is a source-qualified modeling choice, not a proof of a continuum path integral or a result about nature.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-51.pages.json:51:        "statement": "Let $V=W=\\mathbb C$, let $k=N=1$, and consider scalar ADHM data $(b_1,b_2,i,j)\\in\\mathbb C^4$ satisfying $ij=0$ and the stability condition $i\\ne0$. Let $GL(V)=\\mathbb C^\\times$ act by $(b_1,b_2,i,j)\\mapsto(b_1,b_2,\\lambda i,\\lambda^{-1}j)$, and let $T=(\\mathbb C^\\times)^2\\times\\mathbb C^\\times_h$ act by $(t_1,t_2,h)\\cdot(b_1,b_2,i,j)=(t_1b_1,t_2b_2,h^{-1}i,t_1t_2h j)$. Write $X$ for the geometric quotient of the stable locus. Extend $T$ by a flavor torus $\\mathbb C_m^\\times$ acting trivially on $X$, with additive character $\\widetilde m$. The quotient is isomorphic to $\\mathbb C^2$ with coordinates $b_1,b_2$, the fixed locus is the origin, and the tangent weights there are $\\epsilon_1=c_1(t_1)$ and $\\epsilon_2=c_1(t_2)$. If the localized Borel–Moore pushforward of the complex-oriented fundamental class with insertion $c_2^{T_m}(TX\\otimes L_{-1})$ is defined, where $L_{-1}$ is the inverse flavor line of weight $-\\widetilde m$, then the charge-one coefficient is\n$$I_{1,1}^{\\mathrm{adj}}(\\widetilde m)=\\frac{(\\epsilon_1-\\widetilde m)(\\epsilon_2-\\widetilde m)}{\\epsilon_1\\epsilon_2}.$$\nAt charge zero the quotient is a point with rank-zero insertion and coefficient $1$."
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-51.pages.json:103:        "statement": "Fix integers $N\\geq2$ and $k\\geq0$ and a real stability parameter $\\zeta>0$. Let $V=\\mathbb C^k$ and $W=\\mathbb C^N$ with Hermitian structures. The positive-FI stable complex ADHM quotient $X_{k,N}^\\zeta$ is the geometric quotient by $GL(V)$ of quadruples $(B_1,B_2,I,J)$ with $B_i\\in\\operatorname{End}_{\\mathbb C}(V)$, $I:W\\to V$, and $J:V\\to W$, satisfying $[B_1,B_2]+IJ=0$ and the condition that the vectors $B_{i_1}\\cdots B_{i_m}I(w)$ span $V$ for all words of length $m\\geq0$ and $w\\in W$. This is the positive stability chamber; it is not, by definition, the zero-FI regular quotient or the moduli space of anti-self-dual connections.\n\nLet $T_{\\mathbb C}=(\\mathbb C^\\times)^2\\times(\\mathbb C^\\times)^N$ act algebraically, and let $K=(S^1)^2\\times(S^1)^N$ be its maximal compact subgroup. The action is $(t_1,t_2,h)\\cdot(B_1,B_2,I,J)=(t_1B_1,t_2B_2,Ih^{-1},t_1t_2hJ)$, where $h$ acts on $W$ with weights $e_\\alpha^{-1}$. Thus $\\mathbf W=\\sum_{\\alpha=1}^N e_\\alpha^{-1}$ and $\\mathbf W^\\vee=\\sum_{\\alpha=1}^N e_\\alpha$. Write $\\epsilon_i=c_1^K(t_i)$ and $a_\\alpha=c_1^K(e_\\alpha)$; each has mass dimension one in units $c=\\hbar=1$. Let $q$ be a dimensionless formal charge-counting variable.\n\nAssume the complexification of this action gives each $X_{k,N}^\\zeta$ a smooth separated finite-type complex structure of dimension $2kN$, a finite invariant affine atlas, and a finite proper fixed locus $F_{k,N}$. Extend the complex torus to $T_m=T_{\\mathbb C}\\times\\mathbb C_m^\\times$ with maximal compact subgroup $K_m=K\\times S_m^1$; the flavor factor acts trivially on $X_{k,N}^\\zeta$. Let $\\widetilde m$ be the degree-two equivariant first Chern class of its defining flavor character, let $L_{-1}$ be the associated line of inverse flavor character, and set\n$$\\mathcal E_{\\widetilde m}=c_{2kN}^{T_m}(TX_{k,N}^\\zeta\\otimes L_{-1})\\in H_{T_m}^{4kN}(X_{k,N}^\\zeta;\\mathbb Q).$$\nAt a fixed point with tangent weights $w_1,\\ldots,w_{2kN}$, its restriction is $\\prod_{j=1}^{2kN}(w_j-\\widetilde m)$. Let $S_{T_m}$ be generated by the first Chern classes of nontrivial integral characters of $T_m$, put $R_{T_m}=H^*(BK_m;\\mathbb Q)=\\mathbb Q[\\epsilon_1,\\epsilon_2,a_1,\\ldots,a_N,\\widetilde m]$, and let $K_{N,m}=\\operatorname{Frac}(R_{T_m})$. When the character-localized Borel–Moore concentration map and proper fixed-locus trace define $p_{k,*}^{\\mathrm{loc}}$, set\n$$I_{k,N}^{\\mathrm{adj}}(\\widetilde m)=p_{k,*}^{\\mathrm{loc}}\\bigl(\\mathcal E_{\\widetilde m}\\cap[X_{k,N}^\\zeta]_{K_m}\\bigr)\\in S_{T_m}^{-1}R_{T_m}\\subset K_{N,m}.$$\nThis is a localized pushforward, not an ordinary pushforward along a nonproper map. The concentration isomorphism and trace are required inputs and are not asserted to exist by this definition.\n\nWhen these data are supplied for every $k\\geq0$, define the coefficientwise formal series\n$$Z_{N,\\mathrm{adj}}^{\\mathrm{loc}}(q;\\widetilde m)=\\sum_{k\\geq0}q^k I_{k,N}^{\\mathrm{adj}}(\\widetilde m)\\in K_{N,m}\\langle\\!\\langle q\\rangle\\!\\rangle.$$\nIt has no asserted analytic convergence. In the source's Euclidean convention the real mass $m_E$ has mass dimension one, $m=i m_E$, and the equivariant mass is $\\widetilde m=m+(\\epsilon_1+\\epsilon_2)/2$. For the adopted round $S^4_r$ model, $N=2$, $\\epsilon_1=\\epsilon_2=r^{-1}$, and hence $\\widetilde m=i m_E+r^{-1}$. This mass shift is a source-qualified convention. The S4 postulate assumes a supersymmetry-compatible noncommutative regulator agreeing near each pole with the flat-space regulator; it does not construct that regulator or a continuum path-integral measure.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-51.pages.json:161:        "statement": "Fix $N\\geq2$, $k\\geq0$, and $\\zeta>0$. Let $X_{k,N}^\\zeta$ be the positive-FI stable complex ADHM quotient with rotation and dual-framing torus $T=(S^1)^2\\times(S^1)^N$ acting by $(t_1,t_2,h)\\cdot(B_1,B_2,I,J)=(t_1B_1,t_2B_2,Ih^{-1},t_1t_2hJ)$, where $\\mathbf W=\\sum_{\\alpha=1}^N e_\\alpha^{-1}$. Write $\\epsilon_i=c_1^T(t_i)$ and $a_\\alpha=c_1^T(e_\\alpha)$, and assume these weights are algebraically independent. Let $q$ be dimensionless and formal. For an $N$-tuple of partitions $\\vec Y=(Y_1,\\ldots,Y_N)$, each $Y_\\alpha$ a finite downward-closed subset of $\\mathbb Z_{\\geq0}^2$, put $|\\vec Y|=\\sum_\\alpha|Y_\\alpha|$, $\\mathbf V_{\\vec Y}=\\sum_{\\alpha=1}^N e_\\alpha\\sum_{(i,j)\\in Y_\\alpha}t_1^it_2^j$, and $\\mathbf W^\\vee=\\sum_\\alpha e_\\alpha$. Assume the complexified torus acts algebraically on a smooth separated finite-type variety of complex dimension $2kN$, with a finite invariant affine atlas and finite proper fixed locus. Assume the fixed points and tangent characters are exactly those of the positive-FI ADHM theorem. Extend $T$ by a flavor circle acting trivially on the quotient, with additive character $\\widetilde m$, and let $\\mathcal E_{\\widetilde m}=c_{2kN}^{T_m}(TX_{k,N}^\\zeta\\otimes L_{-1})$ be the top Chern insertion for the inverse flavor line. Assume its localized Borel–Moore pushforward is defined by the proper-fixed-locus construction and satisfies the noncompact localization formula. For the source-reported N=2* convention, set $m=i m_E$ for real Euclidean mass $m_E$ and $\\widetilde m=m+(\\epsilon_1+\\epsilon_2)/2$.\n\nAt a fixed point $\\vec Y$, the tangent character is\n$$\\operatorname{ch}_T(T_{\\vec Y}X_{k,N}^\\zeta)=\\mathbf V_{\\vec Y}^{\\vee}\\mathbf V_{\\vec Y}(t_1+t_2-1-t_1t_2)+\\mathbf V_{\\vec Y}^{\\vee}\\mathbf W^\\vee+t_1t_2\\mathbf W\\mathbf V_{\\vec Y}.$$\nFor each tangent character $\\chi$, write $w_\\chi=c_1^T(\\chi)$ and let $E_{\\vec Y}=\\prod_\\chi w_\\chi$ and $N_{\\vec Y}(\\widetilde m)=\\prod_\\chi(w_\\chi-\\widetilde m)$, with multiplicities. Then the mass-inserted coefficient and formal series are\n$$I_{k,N}^{\\mathrm{adj}}(\\widetilde m)=\\sum_{|\\vec Y|=k}\\frac{N_{\\vec Y}(\\widetilde m)}{E_{\\vec Y}},\\qquad Z_{N,\\mathrm{adj}}^{\\mathrm{loc}}(q;\\widetilde m)=\\sum_{k\\geq0}q^k\\sum_{|\\vec Y|=k}\\frac{N_{\\vec Y}(\\widetilde m)}{E_{\\vec Y}}\\in\\mathbb Q(\\epsilon_1,\\epsilon_2,a_1,\\ldots,a_N,\\widetilde m)\\langle\\!\\langle q\\rangle\\!\\rangle.$$\n\nFor the adopted round $S^4_r$ N=2* model, specialize to $N=2$, $\\epsilon_1=\\epsilon_2=r^{-1}$ and $\\widetilde m=i m_E+r^{-1}$, with $q=e^{2\\pi i\\tau}$ on the model's continued Coulomb contour. The source assumes, but does not construct, the supersymmetry-compatible noncommutative S4 regulator agreeing near each pole with the flat-space regulator. The displayed identity is a formal fixed-point calculation for the regulated local factor; it does not prove existence of that regulator, a continuum functional measure, the full S4 partition function, or convergence of the series."
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-51.pages.json:212:        "statement": "For the explicit $k=N=1$ stable scalar ADHM quotient and generic rotation weights with $\\epsilon_1\\epsilon_2\\ne0$, extend the rotation torus by a flavor circle with additive mass $\\widetilde m$. The unique fixed point has tangent weights $\\epsilon_1,\\epsilon_2$, so the charge-one contribution of the top-Chern-class insertion for the inverse flavor line is\n$$q I_{1,1}^{\\mathrm{adj}}(\\widetilde m)=q\\frac{(\\epsilon_1-\\widetilde m)(\\epsilon_2-\\widetilde m)}{\\epsilon_1\\epsilon_2}.$$\nThe diagram shows the unique one-box partition, with the first coordinate horizontal for $B_1$ and the second vertical for $B_2$. This is a finite formal rank-one calculation only; the adopted S4 postulate in this batch is specifically $U(2)$.\n\n~~~tikz\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.9cm,y=0.9cm]\n  \\draw[step=1,gray] (0,0) grid (1,1);\n  \\draw[fill=blue!20] (0,0) rectangle (1,1);\n  \\node at (0.5,0.5) {$Y_1=\\square$};\n  \\draw[->] (0,0) -- (1.6,0) node[right] {$i\\;(B_1)$};\n  \\draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,1.6) node[above] {$j\\;(B_2)$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n~~~"
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-51.pages.json:253:        "statement": "Take $N=2$ and charge $k=1$ in the mass-inserted formal ADHM series. Let $s=\\epsilon_1+\\epsilon_2$, $d=a_2-a_1$, and $\\widetilde m=i m_E+s/2$ in the source's Euclidean mass convention. Exactly one box lies in either color, so the fixed-point tuples are $(\\square,\\varnothing)$ and $(\\varnothing,\\square)$. Their tangent characters and Euler classes are\n$$\\operatorname{ch}_T(T_{(\\square,\\varnothing)}X)=t_1+t_2+\\frac{e_2}{e_1}+t_1t_2\\frac{e_1}{e_2}$$\n$$E_1=\\epsilon_1\\epsilon_2d(s-d),\\qquad N_1=(\\epsilon_1-\\widetilde m)(\\epsilon_2-\\widetilde m)(d-\\widetilde m)(s-d-\\widetilde m)$$\n$$\\operatorname{ch}_T(T_{(\\varnothing,\\square)}X)=t_1+t_2+\\frac{e_1}{e_2}+t_1t_2\\frac{e_2}{e_1}$$\n$$E_2=-\\epsilon_1\\epsilon_2d(s+d),\\qquad N_2=(\\epsilon_1-\\widetilde m)(\\epsilon_2-\\widetilde m)(-d-\\widetilde m)(s+d-\\widetilde m).$$\nFor generic weights satisfying $\\epsilon_1\\epsilon_2d(s-d)(s+d)\\ne0$, the coefficient of $q$ is\n$$\\frac{N_1}{E_1}+\\frac{N_2}{E_2}=\\frac{2(\\epsilon_1-\\widetilde m)(\\epsilon_2-\\widetilde m)(\\widetilde m^2-\\widetilde m s+s^2-d^2)}{\\epsilon_1\\epsilon_2(s^2-d^2)}.$$\nFor the adopted $S^4_r$ model, set $\\epsilon_1=\\epsilon_2=r^{-1}$ and $\\widetilde m=i m_E+r^{-1}$. At $m_E=0$ the displayed one-instanton coefficient vanishes because its first two numerator factors are zero; this is only the degree-one check of the N=4 massless limit.\n\nThe diagram places the single box in each of the two possible framing colors. The first coordinate is horizontal and the second vertical.\n\n~~~tikz\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.8cm,y=0.8cm]\n  \\draw[step=1,gray] (0,0) grid (1,1);\n  \\draw[fill=blue!20] (0,0) rectangle (1,1);\n  \\node[below] at (0.5,0) {$Y_1=\\square$};\n  \\node[below] at (2.1,0) {$Y_2=\\varnothing$};\n  \\node at (3.2,0.5) {$\\longleftrightarrow$};\n  \\draw[step=1,gray] (4,0) grid (5,1);\n  \\draw[fill=orange!20] (4,0) rectangle (5,1);\n  \\node[below] at (4.5,0) {$Y_2=\\square$};\n  \\node[below] at (6.1,0) {$Y_1=\\varnothing$};\n  \\node[above] at (0.5,1.2) {color 1};\n  \\node[above] at (4.5,1.2) {color 2};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n~~~"
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-50.pages.json:49:        "statement": "\nUse natural units $c=\\hbar=1$. For $r>0$, let\n\n$$S^4_r=\\{y\\in\\mathbb R^5:\\sum_{j=1}^5 y_j^2=r^2\\}$$\n\nwith its standard orientation, round metric, scalar curvature $12/r^2$, and standard spin structure. In stereographic length coordinates $x^\\mu$, $\\mu=1,\\ldots,4$, use\n\n$$ds^2=\\frac{\\sum_{\\mu=1}^4 (dx^\\mu)^2}{(1+x^2/(4r^2))^2},\\qquad x^2=\\sum_{\\mu=1}^4(x^\\mu)^2.$$\n\nLet $G=U(2)$, with Lie algebra $\\mathfrak u(2)=\\{X\\in M_2(\\mathbb C):X^\\dagger=-X\\}$ and positive invariant form $(X,Y)=-\\operatorname{tr}_{\\mathbf 2}(XY)$. A gauge sector is a smooth right principal $U(2)$ bundle $P\\to S^4_r$ up to smooth bundle isomorphism, with a smooth connection $A$ and curvature $F_A=dA+A\\wedge A$ in a local trivialization. Fields in the adjoint representation are sections of the associated adjoint bundle. This postulate does not classify the bundle sectors. \n\nThe microscopic field content is one four-dimensional rigid $\\mathcal N=2$ vector multiplet and one adjoint hypermultiplet: the vector multiplet has the connection, one complex adjoint scalar, two adjoint Weyl fermions, and an $SU(2)_R$ auxiliary triplet; the hypermultiplet has two complex adjoint scalars and one adjoint Dirac fermion. There is no other matter. \n\nThe hypermultiplet has real Euclidean mass $m_E$ of mass dimension one, supplied by a background flavor-vector multiplet. \n\nIn the source's orthonormal basis for its four scalar directions, write its mass matrix as $M_{ij}=i m_EJ_{ij}$, where $J$ is the fixed real antisymmetric self-dual complex structure with $J^2=-I$ and source normalization $J_{ij}J^{ij}=4$. This is the $\\mathcal N=2^*$ model; $m_E=0$ is its $\\mathcal N=4$ limit. \n\nAdopt the source's Euclidean curvature-coupled action and Scherk–Schwarz mass deformation, with gauge kinetic normalization\n\n$$S_{\\mathrm{YM}}=\\frac{1}{2g_{\\mathrm{YM}}^2} \\int_{S^4_r}d^4x\\sqrt g\\,(F_{\\mu\\nu},F^{\\mu\\nu}),$$\n\nwhere $g_{\\mathrm{YM}}>0$ is dimensionless and the trace is in the defining representation. \n\nThe theta angle $\\theta$ is real and dimensionless. Set\n\n$$\\tau=\\frac{4\\pi i}{g_{\\mathrm{YM}}^2}+\\frac{\\theta}{2\\pi},\\qquad q=e^{2\\pi i\\tau}.$$\n\nThe rigid background data are a fixed metric, spin structure and connection, trivial $SU(2)_R$ background bundle with zero connection, the mass background, and a nonzero conformal Killing-spinor pair $(\\epsilon,\\widetilde\\epsilon)$ in the source's gamma-matrix convention satisfying\n\n$$\\nabla_\\mu\\epsilon=\\widetilde\\Gamma_\\mu\\widetilde\\epsilon,\\qquad \\nabla_\\mu\\widetilde\\epsilon=-\\frac{1}{4r^2}\\Gamma_\\mu\\epsilon.$$\n\nChoose the source's $\\mathcal N=2$ projection $\\Gamma_{5678}\\epsilon=\\epsilon$ and spinors with $\\widetilde\\epsilon\\epsilon=0$. The selected off-shell supercharge $Q$ uses the source's seven auxiliary scalars and transformations (2.26)–(2.30). With $v^\\mu=\\epsilon\\Gamma^\\mu\\epsilon$, its square on the vector multiplet is the lifted symmetry\n\n$$Q^2=-\\mathcal L_v-\\delta_{\\mathrm{gauge}}(v^M A_M)-\\delta_R-\\delta_\\Omega,$$\n\n where $\\mathcal L_v$ is the lifted Lie derivative, $\\delta_R$ is the rotation generated by $R_{AB}=2\\widetilde\\epsilon\\widetilde\\Gamma_{AB} \\epsilon$, and $\\delta_\\Omega$ is the dilation proportional to $2\\widetilde\\epsilon\\epsilon=0$. On hypermultiplet fields the mass deformation adds the source's flavor rotation: $-v^0M_{ij}\\Phi_j$ on its four scalar components and $-\\tfrac14v^0M_{ij}\\Gamma^{ij}\\chi$ on its fermion, where $M_{ij}$ is the specified mass matrix. The vector $v$ is the round-sphere isometry rotating the two orthogonal coordinate planes with equal angular magnitudes $r^{-1}$, so the pole equivariant parameters are $\\epsilon_1=\\epsilon_2=r^{-1}$. \n\nAdopt the source's formal gauge-fixing and ghost complex. On its gauge-fixing slice $d^*A=0$, the Faddeev–Popov operator is $d^*\\nabla_A$ and the determinant $\\det'(d^*\\nabla_A)$ omits its zero modes; constant gauge transformations are factored by the gauge-group volume. The BRST-like differential $\\delta$ acts by $\\delta X=-[c,X]$, $\\delta c=-a_0-\\tfrac12[c,c]$, $\\delta\\widetilde c=b$, and $\\delta a_0=0$, where $c,\\widetilde c$ are the ghost and antighost, $b$ is the multiplier, and $a_0$ is the constant ghost for ghost. The source adds constant variables $\\widetilde a_0,b_0,c_0,\\widetilde c_0$ to handle the zero-mode sector and uses $Q=s+\\delta$; its square is the constant gauge transformation together with the selected $U(1)$ rotation. This is a formal source prescription, not a construction of a global gauge-fixing slice or measure on every bundle. \n\nIn the Euclidean contour, gauge and hypermultiplet fields take values in the real Lie algebra; the scalar descending from the time component is continued as $\\Phi_0=i\\Phi_0^E$, the seven localization auxiliaries as $K_i=iK_i^E$, and $\\Phi_0^E,K_i^E$ are real Lie-algebra-valued fields. The finite-dimensional variable $a$ in the matrix integral uses the source's continued Cartan contour. Let $\\Sigma$ denote the chosen noncommutative $\\mathbb R^4$ regulator for the instanton sectors, with its $U(1)^2$ rotations and the above $\\epsilon_i$. Adopt the additional source assumption that a supersymmetry-compatible noncommutative deformation on $S^4_r$ exists and agrees near each pole with Nekrasov's noncommutative $\\mathbb R^4$ deformation. Pestun explicitly does not construct this $S^4$ deformation. The gauge-fixed functional measure, contour, and this regulator extension remain formal model inputs; this postulate proves no measure existence, anomaly cancellation, regulator-removal limit, continuum Q-exact identity, or empirical claim.",
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-batch-50.pages.json:106:        "statement": "\nUse the adopted round-$S^4_r$ $\\mathcal N=2^*$ $U(2)$ model with one adjoint hypermultiplet of real Euclidean mass $m_E$, dimensionless coupling $g_{\\mathrm{YM}}>0$, theta angle $\\theta$, and natural units $c=\\hbar=1$. Thus $[r]=\\mathrm{mass}^{-1}$, while $m_E$, Cartan parameters, and $\\epsilon_i$ have mass dimension one; $\\tau=4\\pi i/g_{\\mathrm{YM}}^2+\\theta/(2\\pi)$ and $q=e^{2\\pi i\\tau}$ are dimensionless. Put\n\n$$\\epsilon_1=\\epsilon_2=\\epsilon=r^{-1},\\qquad \\widetilde m=m_E+\\frac{\\epsilon_1+\\epsilon_2}{2}.$$\n\nFor the fixed-point algebra below, first treat $\\epsilon_1,\\epsilon_2$ and the complexified Coulomb weights as generic equivariant variables. Sum the fixed-point terms as a rational function before imposing the round-sphere values; on the physical contour the source uses $\\epsilon_1=\\epsilon_2=r^{-1}$ and imaginary Nekrasov Coulomb weights. \n\nFor $U(2)$ the nonzero roots are $\\pm\\alpha_{12}$, and the Weyl group is $S_2$. Use Pestun's full Lie-algebra integral convention $a\\in\\mathfrak u(2)$, $(a,a)=-\\operatorname{tr}_{\\mathbf 2}(a^2)$, measure $[da]$, and gauge-volume factor $\\operatorname{vol}(U(2))$. Its Cartan rewrite uses the source's Weyl factor $\\Delta(a)=\\prod_{\\alpha\\in\\mathrm{Roots}(U(2))}\\alpha\\cdot a$. The $\\mathfrak u(2)$ expression below is the displayed convention, so no separate $1/|S_2|$ is inserted there. \n\nWrite the real Cartan integration parameters in the source convention as $a_E=(a_1^E,a_2^E)$. The one-loop determinant is evaluated at the analytically continued argument $ia_E$, while the Nekrasov factor uses complexified Coulomb weights $a_\\alpha^{\\mathrm P}=i a_\\alpha^E$, as in the source's continuation. Thus $\\alpha_{12}(a_E)=a_1^E-a_2^E$ in the one-loop product and $\\alpha_{12}(a^{\\mathrm P})=i(a_1^E-a_2^E)$ in the instanton series. \n\nThe source's regularized one-loop factor is\n\n$$Z_{1\\text{-loop}}^{\\mathcal N=2^*}(i a_E;m_E)= \\prod_{\\alpha\\in\\mathrm{Roots}(U(2))}\\prod_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\left[\\frac{((\\alpha\\cdot a_E)^2+\\epsilon^2n^2)^2} {((\\alpha\\cdot a_E+m_E)^2+\\epsilon^2n^2) ((\\alpha\\cdot a_E-m_E)^2+\\epsilon^2n^2)}\\right]^{n/2}.$$\n\nIt is regularized by the source's Barnes-$G$ prescription after dropping the $a_E$-independent divergent factor. If the integral is rewritten on a Cartan, the Weyl factor is included once, separately from this root product. \n\nFor a partition $Y$, use a finite downward-closed subset of $\\mathbb Z_{\\geq0}^2$ and write $|Y|$ for its box count. The source labels its boxes from $1$; translate $(i,j)$ to $(i-1,j-1)$ for the ADHM theorem, which does not change arm or leg lengths. If $s=(i,j)$ in the source's convention, let $\\nu_i(Y)$ be the height of column $i$ and $\\nu'_j(Y)$ the length of row $j$, and define $v_Y(s)=\\nu_i(Y)-j$ and $h_Y(s)=\\nu'_j(Y)-i$; these relative vertical and horizontal lengths may be negative when $s$ is outside $Y$. Let $\\vec Y=(Y_1,Y_2)$, $|\\vec Y|=|Y_1|+|Y_2|$, and let $a_\\alpha^{\\mathrm A}=c_1^T(e_\\alpha)$ be the additive dual-framing weights in the ADHM convention. Let $a_\\alpha^{\\mathrm P}$ be Pestun's Coulomb weights in the Nekrasov factor. \n\nThe exact convention match used here is $a_\\alpha^{\\mathrm A}=-a_\\alpha^{\\mathrm P}$: the ADHM framing representation has character $W=\\sum_\\alpha e_\\alpha^{-1}$, whereas Pestun's Cartan element acts in the fundamental representation. \n\nIn the source convention define\n\n$$E_{\\alpha\\beta}(s)=-h_{Y_\\beta}(s)\\epsilon_1+(v_{Y_\\alpha}(s)+1)\\epsilon_2+a_\\beta^{\\mathrm P}-a_\\alpha^{\\mathrm P}.$$\n\nThe source's formal N=2* instanton factor is\n\n$$Z_{\\mathrm{inst}}^{\\mathcal N=2^*}(a_1^{\\mathrm P},a_2^{\\mathrm P},\\widetilde m;\\epsilon_1,\\epsilon_2,q)= \\sum_{\\vec Y}q^{|\\vec Y|} \\prod_{\\alpha,\\beta=1}^{2}\\prod_{s\\in Y_\\alpha} \\frac{(E_{\\alpha\\beta}(s)-\\widetilde m) (\\epsilon_1+\\epsilon_2-E_{\\alpha\\beta}(s)-\\widetilde m)} {E_{\\alpha\\beta}(s)(\\epsilon_1+\\epsilon_2-E_{\\alpha\\beta}(s))}.$$\n\nPestun reports the following formal partition-function expression:\n\n$$Z_{S^4}^{\\mathrm{formal}}= \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{vol}(U(2))}\\int_{\\mathfrak u(2)}[da]\\, \\exp\\!\\left[-\\frac{8\\pi^2r^2}{g_{\\mathrm{YM}}^2}(a,a)\\right] Z_{1\\text{-loop}}^{\\mathcal N=2^*}(ia;m_E) \\left|Z_{\\mathrm{inst}}^{\\mathcal N=2^*} (ia,\\widetilde m;\\epsilon_1=r^{-1},\\epsilon_2=r^{-1},q)\\right|^2.$$\n\nThe absolute square denotes the north-pole instanton factor times the south-pole anti-instanton factor on the physical real slice; after complex continuation the two factors are kept independent. \n\nIf the noncompact equivariant pushforward and pole-matching assumption stated below is supplied, the charge-one coefficient of the ADHM formal series is\n\n$$C_1= \\frac{(\\epsilon_1-\\widetilde m)(\\epsilon_2-\\widetilde m) (a_2^{\\mathrm P}-a_1^{\\mathrm P}+\\epsilon_1+\\epsilon_2-\\widetilde m) (a_1^{\\mathrm P}-a_2^{\\mathrm P}-\\widetilde m)} {\\epsilon_1\\epsilon_2(a_2^{\\mathrm P}-a_1^{\\mathrm P}+\\epsilon_1+\\epsilon_2) (a_1^{\\mathrm P}-a_2^{\\mathrm P})} + \\frac{(a_1^{\\mathrm P}-a_2^{\\mathrm P}+\\epsilon_1+\\epsilon_2-\\widetilde m) (a_2^{\\mathrm P}-a_1^{\\mathrm P}-\\widetilde m) (\\epsilon_1-\\widetilde m)(\\epsilon_2-\\widetilde m)} {(a_1^{\\mathrm P}-a_2^{\\mathrm P}+\\epsilon_1+\\epsilon_2) (a_2^{\\mathrm P}-a_1^{\\mathrm P})\\epsilon_1\\epsilon_2}.$$\n\nThis is a source-reported formal calculation with a conditional mathematical one-instanton coefficient, not a locally proved continuum path-integral identity. \n\nThe charge-one partition tuples are exactly $((\\Box),\\varnothing)$ and $(\\varnothing,(\\Box))$. \n\nEach square below is one box $(0,0)$ in the color named beneath it; an empty color has no box. The two cases exhaust total size one and the algebraic verification does not rely on the diagram. \n\n~~~tikz\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.8cm,y=0.8cm]\n  \\draw[fill=blue!15] (0,0) rectangle (1,1);\n  \\draw (0,0) grid (1,1);\n  \\node[below] at (0.5,0) {$Y_1=\\Box$};\n  \\node[below] at (2,0) {$Y_2=\\varnothing$};\n  \\draw[fill=orange!20] (4,0) rectangle (5,1);\n  \\draw (4,0) grid (5,1);\n  \\node[below] at (4.5,0) {$Y_2=\\Box$};\n  \\node[below] at (6,0) {$Y_1=\\varnothing$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n~~~",
items/def-emx-lorentz-star.md:62:Let $V=\mathbb R^4$ with ordered coframe $e^0=dx^0,e^1=dx^1,e^2=dx^2,e^3=dx^3$, bilinear form $\eta=\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, inverse $\eta^{-1}=\eta$, and volume form $\mathrm{vol}_\eta=e^0\wedge e^1\wedge e^2\wedge e^3$. For $0\le k\le4$, give $\Lambda^kV^*$ the induced bilinear pairing $\langle\alpha,\beta\rangle_\eta:=\frac1{k!}\alpha_{\mu_1\cdots\mu_k}\beta^{\mu_1\cdots\mu_k}$, with indices raised by $\eta^{-1}$. The Lorentzian Hodge star is the unique linear map $\star_\eta:\Lambda^kV^*\to\Lambda^{4-k}V^*$ satisfying $\alpha\wedge\star_\eta\beta=\langle\alpha,\beta\rangle_\eta\,\mathrm{vol}_\eta$ for all $\alpha,\beta\in\Lambda^kV^*$. For an increasing index set $I\subseteq\{0,1,2,3\}$, write $e^I$ for the corresponding wedge, $I^c$ for its increasing complement, $s(I,I^c)$ for the sign of the concatenation $(I,I^c)$ relative to $(0,1,2,3)$, and $\epsilon_I:=\prod_{i\in I}\epsilon_i$, where $\epsilon_0=-1$ and $\epsilon_1=\epsilon_2=\epsilon_3=1$. The equivalent basis formula is $\star_\eta e^I=\epsilon_I s(I,I^c)e^{I^c}$.
