items/ex-qsm-periodic-ideal-occupation.md:37:Consider two bosonic modes with energies $\epsilon_1,\epsilon_2\in\mathbb R$ in J and inverse temperature $\beta>0$ in J$^{-1}$. Represent occupation outcomes by $(n_1,n_2)\in\mathbb N_0^2$ and define $N_i(n_1,n_2):=n_i$. Under a canonical preparation restricted to total number $N=N_1+N_2=1$, put $w_i=e^{-\beta\epsilon_i}$ and $p_i=w_i/(w_1+w_2)$; then the occupations satisfy $N_1+N_2=1$. For a grand-canonical comparison on these same two modes, choose chemical potential $\mu<\min(\epsilon_1,\epsilon_2)$ in J and put $q_i=e^{-\beta(\epsilon_i-\mu)}\in(0,1)$. The grand law has independent mode occupations, by factorization of its two geometric weights. This is the same product-law mechanism as in the linked periodic ideal model under its trace hypotheses.

